# Primary use case: iperia-back

> **Temporary development section.** These pages are the working notes that shaped Karbone, published as-is for the internal
> review. They will be removed or rewritten before the public release; the reference is the code and the guides.


Repo: /Users/benoit.havret/Projects/iperia/iperia-back (Spring Boot, Kotlin). Verified on CarboneClient.kt and docker-compose.yml.

## Deployment (free on-premise, Community features)

- Self-hosted `carbone/carbone-ee:full-5.8.0-fonts` on port 4000 (docker-compose.yml:21-33, CI service container ci/gitlab-ci.main.kts:759-766).
  Same image for Community and Enterprise; edition decided by `CARBONE_LICENSE` (absent here → Community features).
- Env: `CARBONE_AUTHENTICATION=true` + `CARBONE_AUTHENTICATION_PUBLIC_KEY` (JWT auth, ES512), `CARBONE_TEMPLATE_MANAGEMENT=true`, `CARBONE_LANG=fr`, `CARBONE_TIMEZONE=Europe/Paris`, `CARBONE_EE_STUDIO=true`, `CARBONE_STUDIO_USER`.
- App config: `carbone.client.base-url` (default http://localhost:4000) and `carbone.client.access-token` (optional: token absent → no Authorization header). No `carbone-version` header (not applicable on-premise per Carbone docs).
- Community edition excludes (pricing page): API versioning, template versioning/categories/tags, dynamic images/charts/barcodes/HTML/aggregations/"PDF operations", HA/multi-tenancy, custom auth/storage plugins.

## Integration

- `io.carbone:carbone-sdk:2.0.4` wrapped by `CarboneClient.kt` (service/file/template/carbone). Uses only:
  `addTemplate(absolutePath)`, `render(jsonString, templateId)` (SDK does POST /render + GET /render/{renderId}), `deleteTemplate(id)`.
- Render body always: `{ data: <any object>, enum: {FUNDER_NAME: {...}} (deprecated legacy mapping), convertTo: {formatName:"pdf", formatOptions:{SelectPdfVersion:"3"}} }` → **PDF/A-3 only**.
  Note `SelectPdfVersion` is sent as the string "3" (spec says integer) — works today, SDK should send an integer.
- Body serialized with Jackson (`jacksonObjectMapper`), data is an arbitrary `DATA : Any`.

## Template lifecycle

- Templates are `.docx` stored in OneDrive → downloaded to a temp file → `addTemplate(path)` → id (server SHA-256) kept in a Caffeine cache (1 h, 50 entries) keyed by DocumentTemplate id.
- On cache eviction, a reference-counting listener deletes the remote template only if no other DocumentTemplate maps to the same id (commit 3795a76bd8). Cache invalidated on template update/delete events.
- Only `renderingEngine == CARBONE && generationType == PDF` go through Carbone; legacy XDocReport engine coexists.

## Render consumption

- Sync, bytes written to a temp `File`. Electronic invoices: Carbone PDF/A-3 is then post-processed by Mustang (`ZUGFeRDExporterFromPDFA`) to embed Factur-X XML. Carbone was adopted precisely because XDocReport cannot emit PDF/A-3.

## Resilience / tests

- No retry, no timeout config, no health check. All errors collapsed into `MergeFieldsFileException` via `runCatching`.
- SDK loggers `io.carbone.*` set to OFF (Feign FULL logging noise), wrapper logger INFO.
- Unit tests mock `ICarboneServices` with MockK; integration tests hit the real container.

## Implications for Karbone (must-have for a 1:1 replacement)

1. On-premise first: configurable base URL, **optional** auth token (JWT bearer), no `carbone-version` requirement (send it only when targeting cloud, or make it nullable).
2. `templates.upload(Path | ByteArray | InputStream)` returning the id; `templates.delete(id)`; `render(id, data, options)` returning bytes + filename, sync, hiding the renderId round-trip.
3. `data` as any serializable object: kotlinx `@Serializable` instance, `JsonElement`, `Map`, or a pre-serialized JSON string (iperia serializes with Jackson today → accept a raw JSON string/`JsonElement` escape hatch so Jackson users are not forced into kotlinx annotations).
4. Typed `convertTo` with `PdfOptions(selectPdfVersion = PdfVersion.PDF_A_3)` etc.; `enum`, `lang`, `timezone` per call.
5. Typed errors (template not found vs render failure vs transport) and configurable timeouts; quiet, redacted logging.
6. A test double (`FakeKarbone` / in-memory implementation of the client interface) so consumers can unit test without MockK on internals.
7. Nice-to-have for iperia: hash-first render from bytes (skip the temp file), and a reusable "template cache with reference counting" helper is NOT SDK scope — keep it in the app.
